        <!-- Section 1 -->
        <p class="lead-paragraph">Across the length and breadth of India, an ecological tragedy of unprecedented scale is quietly unfolding beneath the shadow of tower cranes and concrete highways. Wetlands—the indispensable blue-green arteries that have historically buffered Indian towns and cities against floods, recharged subterranean aquifers, filtered municipal waste, and sustained rich biodiversity—are being relentlessly erased. Driven by relentless demographic pressure, speculative real estate expansion, and fragmented environmental governance, rapid and unchecked urbanization has emerged as the single greatest existential threat to India’s aquatic ecosystems.</p>

        <p>Comprehensive ecological surveys and satellite mapping reports indicate that India has lost nearly one-third of its natural wetlands over recent decades. In major metropolitan centers, the trajectory of loss is significantly more severe: cities like Bengaluru, Chennai, Mumbai, Hyderabad, and Guwahati have witnessed between 50% to 90% of their urban water bodies filled in, built over, or degraded into toxic sumps. What was once a sophisticated network of interconnected lakes, seasonal floodplains, lagoons, and marshlands designed by ancient civil engineering traditions has been systematically fragmented into isolated, polluted reservoirs—or buried beneath tarmac and high-rise real estate complexes.</p>

