        <h3>1. Bengaluru: The Dying Lakes of Silicon Valley</h3>
        <p>Once celebrated as the "City of Lakes" with over 280 interconnected water bodies constructed during the Kempe Gowda era, Bengaluru presents the most severe warning of uninhibited urban growth. Driven by the IT boom of the late 1990s and 2000s, the city's built-up area expanded by over 1,000%, while wetland cover plummeted by over 79%.</p>

        <p>Bellandur Lake, the largest water body in the city spanning nearly 800 acres, became world-famous for literally catching fire. Decades of receiving over 400 MLD of untreated sewage containing high concentrations of phosphorus, detergents, and industrial grease led to massive toxic algal blooms and thick layers of flammable chemical foam. In 2015, 2017, and 2018, the lake erupted into towering infernos that burned for hours, sending clouds of acrid, chemical-laden smoke into nearby residential apartments. The destruction of natural drainage channels (<em>rajakaluves</em>) has simultaneously rendered Bengaluru hyper-vulnerable to extreme flash flooding during monsoon rains, turning tech parks into knee-deep rivers.</p>

