        <h3>2. Chennai: The Devastation of Pallikaranai Marshland</h3>
        <p>The southern metropolis of Chennai provides a dramatic illustration of how destroying natural wetlands creates catastrophic urban flood vulnerability. The Pallikaranai marshland, located in the southern suburbs of Chennai, historically acted as a massive natural sponge, absorbing monsoon floodwaters from a catchment area of over 250 square kilometers before releasing them slowly into the Bay of Bengal.</p>

        <p>In 1965, the Pallikaranai marsh covered roughly 5,500 hectares. Today, choked by the IT Corridor (Rajiv Gandhi Salai), mass transit railway lines, government buildings, national institutions, and a sprawling municipal solid waste dumping ground at Perungudi, the intact marshland has shrunk to less than 600 hectares. When torrential rains struck Chennai in December 2015 and again during Cyclone Michaung in late 2023, the floodwaters had nowhere to go. Because the natural wetland sponge had been replaced by concrete foundations, entire residential neighborhoods built over the marsh were submerged under 8 to 10 feet of stagnant water for weeks, causing billions of dollars in damage and tragically claiming human lives.</p>

        <h3>3. East Kolkata Wetlands: Threats to Nature’s Sewage Treatment Plant</h3>
        <p>Stretching across 12,500 hectares on the eastern fringes of Kolkata, the East Kolkata Wetlands (EKW) represent a global ecological marvel and a designated Ramsar site of International Importance. For over a century, these wetlands have treated more than 750 million liters of Kolkata’s daily sewage for free, using a natural combination of sunshine, algae, and microbial action within a complex system of fishponds (known locally as <em>bheris</em>).</p>

        <p>Despite their ecological and economic indispensability—supplying Kolkata with thousands of metric tons of fresh fish and vegetables daily—the EKW is facing immense pressure from real estate syndicates. Driven by the expansion of the Eastern Metropolitan Bypass and Rajarhat-New Town extensions, hundreds of illegal land conversions have taken place. Developers secretly drain <em>bheris</em> at night by severing sewage inlet canals, allowing the ponds to dry up before filling them with soil. If the East Kolkata Wetlands are allowed to collapse, Kolkata will not only lose its natural flood buffer but will also be forced to construct artificial sewage treatment plants costing thousands of crores of rupees in taxpayers' money.</p>

